=== 1. The default procedure: every other item as the anchor ===
Constrain all nine items across sex, free one item at a time, test the
improvement. This is the standard default and requires no choices.

                     item    X2 df  adj_p flag
        1 Little interest 17.16  4 0.0027  DIF
         2 Depressed mood  2.52  4 0.6419    .
         3 Sleep problems  8.11  4 0.1126    .
             4 Low energy 24.09  4 0.0003  DIF
        5 Appetite change 31.87  4 0.0000  DIF
 6 Feeling bad about self  3.04  4 0.6201    .
  7 Trouble concentrating 19.35  4 0.0012  DIF
     8 Psychomotor change 21.47  4 0.0006  DIF
      9 Suicidal ideation 23.20  4 0.0003  DIF

Flagged: 6 of 9 items.
6 of nine items cannot all be the anomaly. When most items carry DIF,
the all-other-item anchor is contaminated by the very items it is meant to
control for: the two groups' scales get linked through biased items, the
common metric is pulled off true, and clean items inherit an apparent
difference. The number to distrust here is 6 itself, not the individual p-values.

That is a mechanism, not yet a finding, and it is not established by this
table. Section 4 of 06 is where it is tested, by planting DIF on a growing
number of items and measuring how often this procedure then blames an
innocent one. Note also which check does NOT support it: on random splits
of this sample the same procedure is conservative, well under nominal,
because a coin-flip split contains no real DIF to contaminate an anchor
with. A control has to contain the thing it is meant to control for.


=== 2. Purified anchor ===
Take the four items with the smallest statistic above as the anchor, free
the rest, then test one studied item at a time against that fixed anchor.
Rank-based anchor selection (Woods 2009). Four is a choice, and section 4
sweeps it rather than defending it.

Anchor set: 2 Depressed mood;  6 Feeling bad about self;  3 Sleep problems;  1 Little interest 

                     item    X2 df  adj_p   flag   SIDS   ESSD
        1 Little interest    NA NA     NA anchor  0.000  0.000
         2 Depressed mood    NA NA     NA anchor  0.000  0.000
         3 Sleep problems    NA NA     NA anchor  0.000  0.000
             4 Low energy 20.13  4 0.0012    DIF -0.064 -0.105
        5 Appetite change 30.48  4 0.0000    DIF -0.050 -0.122
 6 Feeling bad about self    NA NA     NA anchor  0.000  0.000
  7 Trouble concentrating 15.58  4 0.0036    DIF  0.010  0.027
     8 Psychomotor change 16.51  4 0.0030    DIF  0.041  0.153
      9 Suicidal ideation 18.02  4 0.0020    DIF  0.024  0.175

SIDS is the signed difference in expected item score between women and
men at the same latent depression level, in raw points of a 0 to 3 item.
ESSD is the same quantity standardised, so it reads like a Cohen's d.
Sign convention: negative means the item under-reports for women, i.e. at
equal true depression a woman scores lower on this item than a man.

  4 Low energy             X2   20.1  adj p 0.0012  SIDS -0.064 pts  ESSD -0.105  somatic TRUE
  5 Appetite change        X2   30.5  adj p 0.0000  SIDS -0.050 pts  ESSD -0.122  somatic TRUE
  7 Trouble concentrating  X2   15.6  adj p 0.0036  SIDS +0.010 pts  ESSD +0.027  somatic FALSE
  8 Psychomotor change     X2   16.5  adj p 0.0030  SIDS +0.041 pts  ESSD +0.153  somatic TRUE
  9 Suicidal ideation      X2   18.0  adj p 0.0020  SIDS +0.024 pts  ESSD +0.175  somatic FALSE

Largest effect: 4 Low energy at -0.064 item-score points (ESSD -0.105).
Every one of these is statistically unambiguous and small. At n = 5,068 a
test of exact equality answers a question nobody asked; the size is the
answer, and none of these items shifts an expected score by a third of a
point. 06 puts the matching false-positive rate beside them.


